# Agriculture in India: Crops, Seasons, and Farming Systems

> Farming engages close to half of India's workers and decides the food
> security of more than a billion people. This lesson maps the cropping
> seasons, the leading food and cash crops, and the farming systems
> that hold them together.

## 1. Why Agriculture Still Matters

[definition]
**Agriculture** is the art and science of cultivating the soil,
growing crops, and rearing livestock to produce food, fibre, fuel,
and industrial raw materials.
[/definition]

- Agriculture supports the livelihood of nearly half of India's
  workforce and contributes roughly one-sixth of gross value added.
- It supplies raw materials to the sugar, cotton textile, jute, and
  edible-oil industries and feeds the public distribution system.
- A good monsoon and a bumper harvest raise rural demand for consumer
  goods, tractors, and two-wheelers, lifting the whole economy.
- India is among the world's leading producers of rice, wheat,
  sugarcane, cotton, milk, and pulses, and holds one of the largest
  irrigated areas on Earth.

[info]
Because success depends so heavily on the **southwest monsoon** (June
to September), irrigation, weather forecasting, and crop insurance
under PMFBY are central pillars of Indian farm policy.
[/info]

## 2. The Three Cropping Seasons

@diagram tree id="cropping-seasons" layout="tree" aria="Kharif, rabi, and zaid seasons with example crops"
  "Indian cropping seasons" -> Kharif
  "Indian cropping seasons" -> Rabi
  "Indian cropping seasons" -> Zaid
  Kharif -> "Rice, maize, bajra, cotton, jute"
  Rabi -> "Wheat, gram, mustard, barley"
  Zaid -> "Watermelon, cucumber, fodder"
@/diagram

| Season | Months | Main water source | Major crops |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Kharif | June to October | Southwest monsoon | Rice, maize, jowar, bajra, cotton, jute, groundnut, tur |
| Rabi | October to March | Winter rain and irrigation | Wheat, barley, gram, mustard, peas |
| Zaid | April to June | Irrigation | Watermelon, muskmelon, cucumber, vegetables, fodder |

[warning]
A classic mistake is to call wheat a kharif crop because fields look
lush in early summer. Wheat is sown in October-November and harvested
in March-April, so it is strictly a rabi crop.
[/warning]

## 3. Food Crops: Rice, Wheat, Millets, and Pulses

| Crop | Season and needs | Leading producers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Rice | Kharif; about 25 degrees C and over 100 cm rain; flat, water-retentive land | West Bengal, Uttar Pradesh, Punjab |
| Wheat | Rabi; cool 10-15 degrees C while growing and 21-26 degrees C at ripening; 50-75 cm water | Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Punjab |
| Millets (jowar, bajra, ragi) | Hardy nutri-cereals; 50-100 cm rain; tolerate drought and poor soils | Maharashtra, Rajasthan, Karnataka |
| Pulses (tur, gram, moong, urad) | Legumes needing little moisture; fix nitrogen in the soil | Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan, Maharashtra |

[important]
Millets are now promoted as **nutri-cereals** because they are rich in
protein, minerals, and fibre, and they survive on rain-fed land where
rice and wheat would fail — a key answer to climate stress.
[/important]

