## 4. Cash and Plantation Crops

| Crop | Growing conditions | Leading producers | Uses |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Cotton | Black (regur) soil; about 210 frost-free days; 6-8 months in the field | Gujarat, Maharashtra, Telangana | Textiles, oilseed, cattle feed |
| Jute | The golden fibre; hot, humid delta with over 100 cm rain | West Bengal | Gunny bags, carpets, geotextiles |
| Sugarcane | Tropical and subtropical; 75-100 cm rain and a long frost-free season | Uttar Pradesh, Maharashtra | Sugar, gur, molasses, ethanol, bagasse |
| Tea | Well-drained hill slopes; 150-200 cm rain; shade trees | Assam, West Bengal (Darjeeling) | Beverage and major export |
| Coffee | Shade-grown on highlands; Arabica and Robusta varieties | Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil Nadu | Beverage and export |

[example]
Jute thrives only in the Ganga-Brahmaputra delta, where loamy
alluvium, standing water for retting the stalks, and cheap labour
from the crowded countryside come together — which is why West Bengal
grows most of the world's traded jute.
[/example]

## 5. Farming Systems: From Subsistence to Plantation

| System | Key features | Where seen |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Intensive subsistence | Small plots, family labour, high output per hectare | Ganga plain and coastal deltas |
| Commercial grain farming | Mechanised, market-oriented grain on large farms | Punjab, Haryana, western Uttar Pradesh |
| Plantation | Single crop on large estates with capital and on-site processing | Tea in Assam, coffee in Karnataka, rubber in Kerala |
| Shifting cultivation | Slash-and-burn on forest clearings, then fields moved elsewhere | Parts of north-east India (jhum) |
| Dry farming | Moisture-conserving tillage in low-rainfall tracts | Deccan interior |

[tip]
Cropping intensity — taking two or three harvests from the same field
in one year using irrigation and short-duration varieties — is a
favourite exam term; a *ratoon* crop is a second shoot regrown from
the stubble of sugarcane without replanting.
[/tip]

## 6. The Green Revolution and the Revolutions That Followed

From the mid-1960s the **Green Revolution** combined high-yielding
varieties (HYV) of wheat and rice, chemical fertilisers, assured
irrigation, and price support to end recurring food imports. India
moved from ship-to-mouth dependence to comfortable buffer stocks
within about a decade. M. S. Swaminathan led the effort in India,
building on dwarf-wheat work by Norman Borlaug.

| Revolution | Sphere | Starting landmark |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Green | Food grains | HYV package from the mid-1960s |
| White | Milk | Operation Flood, 1970, led by Verghese Kurien from Anand |
| Blue | Fish and aquaculture | Fisheries development programmes |
| Yellow | Oilseeds | Oilseeds Technology Mission of the 1980s |
| Golden | Horticulture and honey | Horticulture missions after the 1990s |

[warning]
The Green Revolution succeeded brilliantly in Punjab, Haryana, and
western Uttar Pradesh but widened regional gaps, and overuse of canal
water and chemicals has caused waterlogging, falling water tables,
and pesticide resistance — the lessons behind today's push for
sustainable farming.
[/warning]

## 7. Challenges and the Road to Sustainable Farming

- Average holdings are tiny — about 1.1 hectares — and shrink with
  each generation, limiting mechanisation and bargaining power.
- Roughly half the net sown area still depends on rainfall alone.
- Soil degradation, groundwater depletion, and stubble burning demand
  conservation practices such as crop rotation and zero tillage.
- Reforms now in play: soil health cards, micro-irrigation (drip and
  sprinkler), crop insurance, e-NAM online mandis, and natural
  farming — Sikkim became India's first fully organic state in 2016.

[important]
The **Minimum Support Price (MSP)** announced for about two dozen
crops gives farmers a guaranteed floor price, while government
procurement of rice and wheat keeps buffer stocks and the ration
system running.
[/important]

## 8. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember this |
| --- | --- |
| Kharif | June-October, monsoon-fed: rice, cotton, jute, maize |
| Rabi | October-March: wheat, gram, mustard |
| Zaid | April-June irrigated season: melons, vegetables, fodder |
| Rice and wheat | West Bengal-UP-Punjab and UP-MP-Punjab |
| Cotton and jute | Black soil of Gujarat-Maharashtra and delta jute of West Bengal |
| Green Revolution | HYV plus fertiliser plus irrigation from the mid-1960s |
| White Revolution | Operation Flood, 1970, Verghese Kurien |
| Biggest challenges | Tiny holdings and dependence on the monsoon |
